Hello everyone,

I had to create a new post. I did not find any post that has already covered the new shiny feature: asynchronous programming.

I tested this new feature and I think I understood how it works. However, I still have an issue. See below of my application's code.

library(shiny)
library(promises)
library(future)
plan(multiprocess)

ui <- fluidPage(
  actionButton("refresh_data","refresh_data"),
  tableOutput("table"),
  sliderInput("obs", "Number of observations", 0, 1000, 500),
  actionButton("goButton", "Go!"),
  plotOutput("distPlot")
)

read.csv.long <- function(url){
    Sys.sleep(60)
    data = read.csv(url)
}
server <- function(input, output, session) {

  observe({
    url = "https://rstudio.github.io/promises/data.csv"

    data <- eventReactive(input$refresh_data, {
      future(read.csv.long(url))
    })

    output$table <- renderTable({
      data() %...>% head(5)
    })

    data2 <- eventReactive(input$goButton, {
      future({
        isolate(rnorm(input$obs))
      })
    })

    output$distPlot <- renderPlot({

       data2() %...>% hist()
    })
  })
}

shinyApp(ui = ui, server = server)

In this application, I have two button, one that allows me to perform a long operation and the other that allows to perform a quick operation. These two operations are executed asynchronously.
My goal is to launch my long operation then during this time to visualize my histogram (short operation). All this on the same web page.
Problem is I can not do it. I have to wait until long operation is completed before I can view my histogram.
On the other hand when I opened my page in a new tab, I can visualize my histogram while long operation is running (but that's not what I want to do)

Is it possible to do that ? Can you help me?

Dear all,

I have an app in where I first point to a directory that contains a GIT repository (this is done in tab1). That directory is stored using a reactive value called "proj_dir()". Later on, I have another tab in which I want to track changes in my GIT folder, for this, I created the following code using personal library to wraps some external functions (mylib) :

# Load repository
git_repo <- reactive({git2r::repository(proj_dir())})

# Check for files that have been not added to the latest commit
files_to_commit<-eventReactive({c(input$f_commits,input$tabs,input$commit)},{
    unname(unlist(git2r::status(git_repo(),all_untracked = TRUE)))
  })

# Print message with pending files
output$status <- renderText({
  if(!is.null(files_to_commit())){
      paste(files_to_commit(),collapse="\n")
  }
  else{
      "No pending files or changes were found"
  }
})

commit_pending<-observeEvent(input$commit,{
  config_repo(git_repo(),input$username,input$useremail)
  mylib::commit_files(git_repo(),files_to_commit(),input$message_commit)
  output$commit_succes<-renderText("All files are up do date")
})

However, I would like the files_to_commit() to reload/recalculate after committing (this is why files_to_commit is event reactive to input$commit amongst others). I haven't been able to make it work in such a way that once I commit, the file_to_commit() is updated, and it  finally triggers the output$statis with the message that no pending files were found. Is there a way to force a reactive expression to be recalculated? I guess that the code inside is not triggered since git_repo() is the same but haven't come with a way to fix it.

Regards,
